{"id":4213,"date":"2017-08-22T22:38:23","date_gmt":"2017-08-23T02:38:23","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/ibafflatusdesign.space\/?p=4213"},"modified":"2019-02-17T19:02:19","modified_gmt":"2019-02-18T00:02:19","slug":"venice-italy","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/brand-blog\/venice-italy\/","title":{"rendered":"Venice, Italy"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Venice was the most picturesque city I have ever been to. Every couple of steps that I took, I was in front of some quaint little building or on a cute little bridge. The city has many styles and a lot of things going on but somehow it all works perfectly together. Venice definitely lives up to the hype!<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A special thanks&nbsp;to the <a rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" href=\"https:\/\/www.ricksteves.com\/watch-read-listen\/audio\/audio-europe\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Rick Steves Audio Europe\u2122 Travel App<\/u><\/a> for providing more info of some of the most iconic sites in Venice.<\/p>\n\n\n<div class=\"su-tabs su-tabs-style-default su-tabs-mobile-stack\" data-active=\"1\" data-scroll-offset=\"0\" data-anchor-in-url=\"no\"><div class=\"su-tabs-nav\"><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">City Tour<\/span><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">Frari Church<\/span><\/div><div class=\"su-tabs-panes\"><div class=\"su-tabs-pane su-u-clearfix su-u-trim\" data-title=\"City Tour\">\n<style type=\"text\/css\">\r\n\t#foogallery-gallery-2548 .fg-image {\r\n        width: 150px;\r\n    }\r\n<\/style>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"foogallery foogallery-container foogallery-default foogallery-lightbox-foobox fg-gutter-10 fg-center fg-default fg-light fg-border-thin fg-shadow-outline fg-loading-default fg-loaded-fade-in fg-hover-fade fg-hover-zoom fg-ready\" id=\"foogallery-gallery-2548\" data-foogallery=\"{&quot;item&quot;:{&quot;showCaptionTitle&quot;:true,&quot;showCaptionDescription&quot;:true},&quot;lazy&quot;:true}\" >\r\n\t<div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2812.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Welcome to Venice! The busy canal that greets you is called the Grand Canal. To see this view, head over to Ponte dell&#039;Accademia.\" data-attachment-id=\"4137\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Welcome to Venice! The busy canal that greets you is called the Grand Canal. To see this view, head over to Ponte dell&#039;Accademia.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2812\/3480711844.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Welcome to Venice! The busy canal that greets you is called the Grand Canal. To see this view, head over to Ponte dell'Accademia.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2722.jpg\" data-attachment-id=\"4152\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2722\/2422846646.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2835.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Saint Mark&#039;s Square is covered with buildings with various types of architecture. The building on the left houses old offices built in early 16th century in column-and-arch Renaissance style. The building on right was built a century later in High Renaissance style. The building under which we are standing was built by Napoleon in Neoclassical style. The campanile is build with simple brick due to the soft and spongy Venetian land. It fell in 1902 and was rebuilt ten years later. The golden angel on top always faces the wind.\" data-attachment-id=\"4145\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Saint Mark&#039;s Square is covered with buildings with various types of architecture. The building on the left houses old offices built in early 16th century in column-and-arch Renaissance style. The building on right was built a century later in High Renaissance style. The building under which we are standing was built by Napoleon in Neoclassical style. The campanile is build with simple brick due to the soft and spongy Venetian land. It fell in 1902 and was rebuilt ten years later. The golden angel on top always faces the wind.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2835\/3416062931.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Saint Mark's Square is covered with buildings with various types of architecture. The building on the left houses old offices built in early 16th century in column-and-arch Renaissance style. The building on right was built a century later in High Renaissance style. The building under which we are standing was built by Napoleon in Neoclassical style. The campanile is build with simple brick due to the soft and spongy Venetian land. It fell in 1902 and was rebuilt ten years later. The golden angel on top always faces the wind.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2843.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Finally, the St. Mark&#039;s Basilica is built in 1063 in a mix a various international styles. Venice was settled in the 5th and 6th centuries as Italians were trying to flee the mainland, which was being taken over by barbarian tribes. In order to make money and sustain themselves, the citizens made Venice a popular trading post. Being the middleman between the East and the West, Venice was exposed to a lot of different cultures. As a result, the Basilica features Roman arches on top of Greek columns, a handful of different spires and typical Muslim domes and mosaics. The four horses above the main entrance were taken from the Byzantine capital, Constantinople, in 1204. Legend has it that Saint Mark&#039;s remains are under the main altar of the church.\" data-attachment-id=\"4146\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Finally, the St. Mark&#039;s Basilica is built in 1063 in a mix a various international styles. Venice was settled in the 5th and 6th centuries as Italians were trying to flee the mainland, which was being taken over by barbarian tribes. In order to make money and sustain themselves, the citizens made Venice a popular trading post. Being the middleman between the East and the West, Venice was exposed to a lot of different cultures. As a result, the Basilica features Roman arches on top of Greek columns, a handful of different spires and typical Muslim domes and mosaics. The four horses above the main entrance were taken from the Byzantine capital, Constantinople, in 1204. Legend has it that Saint Mark&#039;s remains are under the main altar of the church.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2843\/1552819972.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Finally, the St. Mark's Basilica is built in 1063 in a mix a various international styles. Venice was settled in the 5th and 6th centuries as Italians were trying to flee the mainland, which was being taken over by barbarian tribes. In order to make money and sustain themselves, the citizens made Venice a popular trading post. Being the middleman between the East and the West, Venice was exposed to a lot of different cultures. As a result, the Basilica features Roman arches on top of Greek columns, a handful of different spires and typical Muslim domes and mosaics. The four horses above the main entrance were taken from the Byzantine capital, Constantinople, in 1204. Legend has it that Saint Mark's remains are under the main altar of the church.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2844.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"A closer look at the arches, columns and mosaics.\" data-attachment-id=\"4147\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"A closer look at the arches, columns and mosaics.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2844\/4086318165.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">A closer look at the arches, columns and mosaics.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2790.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"To the right of the old offices and next to the Basilica stands the first ever digital clock, which updates the time every five minutes, built in 1500. Under the digital clock is a circular clock that not only tells the time but also the zodiac sign and the moon cycle. At the very top of the clock is the bell that is rung every hour by the two giants on the top (not visible from this angle). Finally, between the digital clock and the bell is the symbol of the city and of Saint Mark: the alert winged lion. This winged lion is a motif present throughout the city.\" data-attachment-id=\"4133\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"To the right of the old offices and next to the Basilica stands the first ever digital clock, which updates the time every five minutes, built in 1500. Under the digital clock is a circular clock that not only tells the time but also the zodiac sign and the moon cycle. At the very top of the clock is the bell that is rung every hour by the two giants on the top (not visible from this angle). Finally, between the digital clock and the bell is the symbol of the city and of Saint Mark: the alert winged lion. This winged lion is a motif present throughout the city.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2790\/1031745455.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">To the right of the old offices and next to the Basilica stands the first ever digital clock, which updates the time every five minutes, built in 1500. Under the digital clock is a circular clock that not only tells the time but also the zodiac sign and the moon cycle. At the very top of the clock is the bell that is rung every hour by the two giants on the top (not visible from this angle). Finally, between the digital clock and the bell is the symbol of the city and of Saint Mark: the alert winged lion. This winged lion is a motif present throughout the city.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2799.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"To the right of the Basilica is the Doge&#039;s Palace, in yet another architectural style. This architectural style if Venetian Gothic, unique to the city. The elements of Venetian Gothic architecture are the three-leaf clover points topped with four-leaf clover medallions. Buildings in this style are also typically top heavy.\n&lt;p&gt;The Doge&#039;s Palace was the home of the Venetian ruler from 1150 to 1550. Unlike any other city in Europe at the time, the Doge was elected by a council of nobles. As a result, he did not fear his people and did not need to build fortifications to protect against them.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"4134\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"To the right of the Basilica is the Doge&#039;s Palace, in yet another architectural style. This architectural style if Venetian Gothic, unique to the city. The elements of Venetian Gothic architecture are the three-leaf clover points topped with four-leaf clover medallions. Buildings in this style are also typically top heavy.\n&lt;p&gt;The Doge&#039;s Palace was the home of the Venetian ruler from 1150 to 1550. Unlike any other city in Europe at the time, the Doge was elected by a council of nobles. As a result, he did not fear his people and did not need to build fortifications to protect against them.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2799\/3989975038.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">To the right of the Basilica is the Doge's Palace, in yet another architectural style. This architectural style if Venetian Gothic, unique to the city. The elements of Venetian Gothic architecture are the three-leaf clover points topped with four-leaf clover medallions. Buildings in this style are also typically top heavy.\n<p>The Doge's Palace was the home of the Venetian ruler from 1150 to 1550. Unlike any other city in Europe at the time, the Doge was elected by a council of nobles. As a result, he did not fear his people and did not need to build fortifications to protect against them.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2771.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The ceiling of the grand staircase that leads visitors to the second floor of the palace.\" data-attachment-id=\"4127\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The ceiling of the grand staircase that leads visitors to the second floor of the palace.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2771\/3142272392.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The ceiling of the grand staircase that leads visitors to the second floor of the palace.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/02\/DSC_2772.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Another ornate ceiling within the palace. This one is in the &lt;i&gt;Sala delle Quattro Porte&lt;\/i&gt; (or the Room with the Four Doors in English). The name for this room comes from the importance given to the four doors made from imported eastern marble, again showing the cities proximity to various international cultures.\" data-attachment-id=\"4194\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Another ornate ceiling within the palace. This one is in the &lt;i&gt;Sala delle Quattro Porte&lt;\/i&gt; (or the Room with the Four Doors in English). The name for this room comes from the importance given to the four doors made from imported eastern marble, again showing the cities proximity to various international cultures.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/02\/DSC_2772\/3425777578.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Another ornate ceiling within the palace. This one is in the <i>Sala delle Quattro Porte<\/i> (or the Room with the Four Doors in English). The name for this room comes from the importance given to the four doors made from imported eastern marble, again showing the cities proximity to various international cultures.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2778.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"This ornate ceiling is part of the Council Chamber. After a fire in 1574, the entire room was redone in this style. The wood paneling was done by Francesco Bello and Andrea da Faenza. The paintings were done by Paolo Veroneze. The three main paintings are &lt;i&gt; Good Government of the Republic&lt;\/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;Triumph of Faith&lt;\/i&gt; and &lt;i&gt;Venice with Justice and Peace&lt;\/i&gt;. 