ore_title'><\/div><img class='fca_qc_score_img' src=''><div class='fca_qc_score_desc'><\/div><\/div> \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t<div class='fca_qc_quiz_footer' style='display: none;'>\r\n\t\t\t\t<span class='fca_qc_question_count'><\/span>\t\t\r\n\t\t\t<\/div>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t<div class='fca_qc_your_answer_container' style='display:none;'><p class='fca_qc_your_answers_text'>Your Answers<\/p><div class='fca_qc_insert_response_above'><\/div><\/div> \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t<\/div>\r\n\t\t\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"su-tabs-pane su-u-clearfix su-u-trim\" data-title=\"Frari Church\">\n<style type=\"text\/css\">\r\n\t#foogallery-gallery-4161 .fg-image {\r\n        width: 150px;\r\n    }\r\n<\/style>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"foogallery foogallery-container foogallery-default foogallery-lightbox-foobox fg-gutter-10 fg-center fg-default fg-light fg-border-thin fg-shadow-outline fg-loading-default fg-loaded-fade-in fg-hover-fade fg-hover-zoom fg-ready\" id=\"foogallery-gallery-4161\" data-foogallery=\"{&quot;item&quot;:{&quot;showCaptionTitle&quot;:true,&quot;showCaptionDescription&quot;:true},&quot;lazy&quot;:true}\" >\r\n\t<div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2740.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Frari Church was built by the international Franciscan order from 1250 to 1443. It is different from most of the churches in Venice in that it is a Gothic church and that it features a T-shaped floor plan instead of a cross-shaped floor plan. In the distance, the colorful altarpiece is visible through an arch in the distance.\" data-attachment-id=\"4153\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Frari Church was built by the international Franciscan order from 1250 to 1443. It is different from most of the churches in Venice in that it is a Gothic church and that it features a T-shaped floor plan instead of a cross-shaped floor plan. In the distance, the colorful altarpiece is visible through an arch in the distance.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2740\/1981832696.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Frari Church was built by the international Franciscan order from 1250 to 1443. It is different from most of the churches in Venice in that it is a Gothic church and that it features a T-shaped floor plan instead of a cross-shaped floor plan. In the distance, the colorful altarpiece is visible through an arch in the distance.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2744.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The altarpiece in the Frari Church is Titian&#039;s &lt;i&gt;Assumption of Mary&lt;\/i&gt; (1518). This painting was very progressive for its time. Featuring a teenage Mary and many colorful and active figures, it contrasted with the more solemn paintings with statue-like figures. The Church was uneasy about purchasing such a modern painting and it wasn&#039;t until the Holy Roman Emperor offered to buy it that the Church accepted to buy the painting from Titian.\n\n&lt;p&gt;Wealthy Venetians, like other Christians around the world, often made significant donations to the church to ensure safe passage for their soul. Some of the largest donations even earned a tomb inside the church. This one on the wall is of Doge Foscari.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"4155\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The altarpiece in the Frari Church is Titian&#039;s &lt;i&gt;Assumption of Mary&lt;\/i&gt; (1518). This painting was very progressive for its time. Featuring a teenage Mary and many colorful and active figures, it contrasted with the more solemn paintings with statue-like figures. The Church was uneasy about purchasing such a modern painting and it wasn&#039;t until the Holy Roman Emperor offered to buy it that the Church accepted to buy the painting from Titian.\n\n&lt;p&gt;Wealthy Venetians, like other Christians around the world, often made significant donations to the church to ensure safe passage for their soul. Some of the largest donations even earned a tomb inside the church. This one on the wall is of Doge Foscari.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2744\/2555585072.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The altarpiece in the Frari Church is Titian's <i>Assumption of Mary<\/i> (1518). This painting was very progressive for its time. Featuring a teenage Mary and many colorful and active figures, it contrasted with the more solemn paintings with statue-like figures. The Church was uneasy about purchasing such a modern painting and it wasn't until the Holy Roman Emperor offered to buy it that the Church accepted to buy the painting from Titian.\n\n<p>Wealthy Venetians, like other Christians around the world, often made significant donations to the church to ensure safe passage for their soul. Some of the largest donations even earned a tomb inside the church. This one on the wall is of Doge Foscari.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2746.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Donatello&#039;s John the Baptist, pictured here in the center, shows the contrast between Florentine and Venetian art. While Venetian art was beautiful and pure, Florentine art featured realism, true human emotions and harsher lines. Here, John the Baptist is either announcing the arrival of Christ or the Renaissance.\" data-attachment-id=\"4156\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Donatello&#039;s John the Baptist, pictured here in the center, shows the contrast between Florentine and Venetian art. While Venetian art was beautiful and pure, Florentine art featured realism, true human emotions and harsher lines. Here, John the Baptist is either announcing the arrival of Christ or the Renaissance.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2746\/4016741844.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Donatello's John the Baptist, pictured here in the center, shows the contrast between Florentine and Venetian art. While Venetian art was beautiful and pure, Florentine art featured realism, true human emotions and harsher lines. Here, John the Baptist is either announcing the arrival of Christ or the Renaissance.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2750.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Elaborate reliquary altar\" data-attachment-id=\"4157\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Elaborate reliquary altar\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2750\/2611341912.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Elaborate reliquary altar<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2757.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The tomb of Titian was built in 1852 to remember the great painter of Venetian origin. Titian is pictured sitting in the middle of the tomb, with a crown of laurels on his head, symbolizing the fact that he was a great artist. Some of his famous paintings are carved into the background of his tomb, including his &lt;i&gt;Assumption of Mary&lt;\/i&gt;, which is directly behind his statue.\n&lt;p&gt;Titian loved his city and always came back to it even if his contemporaries all flocked to Rome. When he got older, he arranged to be buried in the Frari Church. He even started painting a somber Piet\u00e0 for his grave. The Piet\u00e0 was incomplete at the time of his death and finished by Palma Giovane. An engraving of it is located in the top right corner of this tomb.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"4160\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The tomb of Titian was built in 1852 to remember the great painter of Venetian origin. Titian is pictured sitting in the middle of the tomb, with a crown of laurels on his head, symbolizing the fact that he was a great artist. Some of his famous paintings are carved into the background of his tomb, including his &lt;i&gt;Assumption of Mary&lt;\/i&gt;, which is directly behind his statue.\n&lt;p&gt;Titian loved his city and always came back to it even if his contemporaries all flocked to Rome. When he got older, he arranged to be buried in the Frari Church. He even started painting a somber Piet\u00e0 for his grave. The Piet\u00e0 was incomplete at the time of his death and finished by Palma Giovane. An engraving of it is located in the top right corner of this tomb.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2757\/3613708131.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The tomb of Titian was built in 1852 to remember the great painter of Venetian origin. Titian is pictured sitting in the middle of the tomb, with a crown of laurels on his head, symbolizing the fact that he was a great artist. Some of his famous paintings are carved into the background of his tomb, including his <i>Assumption of Mary<\/i>, which is directly behind his statue.\n<p>Titian loved his city and always came back to it even if his contemporaries all flocked to Rome. When he got older, he arranged to be buried in the Frari Church. He even started painting a somber Piet\u00e0 for his grave. The Piet\u00e0 was incomplete at the time of his death and finished by Palma Giovane. An engraving of it is located in the top right corner of this tomb.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2759.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"This monument honors Antonio Canova (1757 -1822), a great Italian sculptor. It was initially intended as Titian&#039;s tomb and the design was instead used for that of an Austrian princess. Canova&#039;s pupils evidently chose to use it for him as well. The triangle shaped is reminiscent of the pharaoh tombs in Egypt and could also be a symbol for the Holy Trinity. What is obvious is that all the figures are shaken up with grief as they make their way to pay their respects. Another interesting aspect of the design is that even the winged lion here is depressed, whereas he was standing tall and proud on Titian&#039;s tomb. Canova&#039;s tomb doesn&#039;t contain his body though, only his heart.\" data-attachment-id=\"4125\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"This monument honors Antonio Canova (1757 -1822), a great Italian sculptor. It was initially intended as Titian&#039;s tomb and the design was instead used for that of an Austrian princess. Canova&#039;s pupils evidently chose to use it for him as well. The triangle shaped is reminiscent of the pharaoh tombs in Egypt and could also be a symbol for the Holy Trinity. What is obvious is that all the figures are shaken up with grief as they make their way to pay their respects. Another interesting aspect of the design is that even the winged lion here is depressed, whereas he was standing tall and proud on Titian&#039;s tomb. Canova&#039;s tomb doesn&#039;t contain his body though, only his heart.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2759\/2754763163.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">This monument honors Antonio Canova (1757 -1822), a great Italian sculptor. It was initially intended as Titian's tomb and the design was instead used for that of an Austrian princess. Canova's pupils evidently chose to use it for him as well. The triangle shaped is reminiscent of the pharaoh tombs in Egypt and could also be a symbol for the Holy Trinity. What is obvious is that all the figures are shaken up with grief as they make their way to pay their respects. Another interesting aspect of the design is that even the winged lion here is depressed, whereas he was standing tall and proud on Titian's tomb. Canova's tomb doesn't contain his body though, only his heart.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2754.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Giovanni Pesaro\u2019s Memorial\" data-attachment-id=\"4159\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Giovanni Pesaro\u2019s Memorial\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2754\/1532679191.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Giovanni Pesaro\u2019s Memorial<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2741.jpg\" data-attachment-id=\"4154\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2741\/1969767964.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2762.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Another one of Titian&#039;s paintings caused controversy in the Frari Church: &lt;i&gt;Madonna di Ca&#039;Pesaro&lt;\/i&gt;. For one, this painting broke the tradition of having Madonna and Child in the middle of the painting to allow Titian to create motion. The painting was commissioned by the Pesaro family, pictured kneeling in the bottom right. On the left, the head of the Pesaro family kneels in front of the Virgin to thank her for his victory (signaled by the laurel on the flag) over the Turks (signaled by the two Turkish prisoners). Meanwhile, two angels are playing with a cross above.\" data-attachment-id=\"4126\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Another one of Titian&#039;s paintings caused controversy in the Frari Church: &lt;i&gt;Madonna di Ca&#039;Pesaro&lt;\/i&gt;. For one, this painting broke the tradition of having Madonna and Child in the middle of the painting to allow Titian to create motion. The painting was commissioned by the Pesaro family, pictured kneeling in the bottom right. On the left, the head of the Pesaro family kneels in front of the Virgin to thank her for his victory (signaled by the laurel on the flag) over the Turks (signaled by the two Turkish prisoners). Meanwhile, two angels are playing with a cross above.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/01\/DSC_2762\/4123640209.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Another one of Titian's paintings caused controversy in the Frari Church: <i>Madonna di Ca'Pesaro<\/i>. For one, this painting broke the tradition of having Madonna and Child in the middle of the painting to allow Titian to create motion. The painting was commissioned by the Pesaro family, pictured kneeling in the bottom right. On the left, the head of the Pesaro family kneels in front of the Virgin to thank her for his victory (signaled by the laurel on the flag) over the Turks (signaled by the two Turkish prisoners). Meanwhile, two angels are playing with a cross above.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/01\/DSC_2752.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"A wall of the Amsterdam Museum proudly displays gable stones that